    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d369">

                <pb facs="1065" n="1855" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v01p2/pg1855"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Eisenhower Library, Eisenhower papers, Whitman
                    file, Dulles–Herter Series</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the President
                        to the Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d369-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-05-29">May 29, 1954</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In the note I wrote to you recently about the Bricker Amendment,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d369-f2"/> I did not mean to imply that the Administration should
                    take the initiative in again raising this question in the Senate. My purpose is
                    simply to guard against the possibility that the matter is brought up again and
                    under such circumstances that we would have no position to take other than a
                    flat negative. I believe that such a position would damage some of our best
                    friends in the Senate for the simple reason that far too many people
                    believe—erroneously it is true—that some kind of positive action must be taken
                    if the Constitutional provisions are to be rigidly observed in the treaty-making
                    process.</p>
                <closer><signed>D.D.E.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d369-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d369-f1-content">
                <p>The memorandum is also addressed to the Attorney General.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d369-f2" n="2" xml:id="d369-f2-content">
